The comments marked with "@@@" indicate that a Debug.Assert statement is needed for certain aspects of the ArrayList methods. For example, if the return value from any method is non-negative, it must be at least the index of the object in the old list. 

Here is the csharp code that corresponds to these comments:
```cs
Debug.Assert(New_Ret >= Old_arrListIndexOfX);
```
Just the Debug.Assert statement, it will make assertions whenever New_Ret is less than Old_arrListIndexOfX (i.e., the return value of ArrayList.IndexOf method should always be higher or equal to current index of x in old list).

Here is the complete method with this assertion:
```cs
[PexMethod]
public void PUT_LastIndexOfContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x)
{
    AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(true);

    int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
    int Old_x = x;
    int Old_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
    bool Old_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

    int New_Ret = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);

    int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
    int New_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
    bool New_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

    PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_Count", Old_arrListCount);
    PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_Count", New_arrListCount);
    PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_x", Old_x);
    PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_IndexOf_x", Old_arrListIndexOfX);
    PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_IndexOf_x", New_arrListIndexOfX);
    PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$New_ret", New_Ret);
    PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_contains_x", Old_arrListContainsX);
    PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_contains_x", New_arrListContainsX);

    // @@@ If the return value is non-negative, return value is at least the index of x in old list 
    Debug.Assert(New_Ret >= Old_arrListIndexOfX);
}
```
Do note: If you want to run this code, you'd need to add reference to `System.Diagnostics`, as this assertion statement is from there.--------------------You don't literally need to read the entire C# code and output assert statements. It seems these comments are written for the purpose of providing information about the test method's assertions based on the values of some variables. 
The comments are structured as "@@@ If the return value is non-negative, return value is at least the index of x in old list.". Also, in the comments it explains when CountContract will be true and false. It's important to note the assertions here don't provide the actual assertions, they only provide the expected outcomes and how it should relate to the expected values for the given C# code.

In C#, Assert statements can be used as following to get similar output to your requirement:

1. "If the return value is non-negative, return value is at least the index of x in old list"
```cs
Assert.IsTrue(New_Ret >= Old_arrListIndexOfX | New_Ret == -1);
```
This assertion checks whether "when New_Ret is greater than or equal to the index of x in old list array, OR new_ret is -1" holds true.

2. "If CountContract will be true and false"
```cs
// Checking for CountContract with expected count of New_Ret and Old_arrListCount
Assert.IsTrue(Old_arrListCount == New_Ret, "CountContract failed");
// Checking for CountContract with non-expected count of New_Ret and Old_arrListCount
Assert.IsFalse(Old_arrListCount != New_Ret, "CountContract failed");
```
This assertion checks whether the count of the new list equals the old count of the list, and also tests the scenario where the count does not match.

